  "account": "This position is about one checker and twenty numbers. You are eighteen pips behind with a man on the bar, so the race is gone; your wins come from a shot later, when X has to bring four checkers off the midpoint and two off the 8-point past whatever you keep back. The checker that wins that game is the one entering now. bar/18 parks it on X's bar point, where any 6 from the midpoint and any 1 from the 8-point hits it, more than half of X's rolls, against a four-point board. Hit there, you are dancing against four points while X comes home freely, and your gammon losses nearly double, from about one game in sixteen to one in nine. bar/18 is a huge blunder, costing more than a quarter of a point. Against a four-point board a blot that gets hit more than half the time is not an outpost; it is a gift.\n\nThe best play is bar/24 8/2. The ace point is safe: X cannot hit loose there without exposing a checker to your five-point board, a gamble X will not take while cruising to a race win. From there the checker covers X's 5-point and bar point directly and the outfield beyond them indirectly, which is where X's midpoint checkers have to land. The 6 then has to go somewhere, and 8/2 is the least damaging place. It keeps the 7-point, whose spare covers the 4-point with 3s and the 2-point with 5s, and it starts the 2-point, which counts in a closeout. bar/24 7/1 is an error, costing about a twelfth of a point: it gives up those builders for an ace point that barely matters when you finally hit. bar/24 13/7 fails for a different reason: it breaks your midpoint and leaves a blot that X's own midpoint checkers hit with 1s.\n\nYour reasoning had the right picture of X's plan and the wrong price for interfering with it. Controlling the outfield from the bar point is real, but small, and it costs the whole game when X rolls first. We checked your idea in its best case, with X's board weakened to three points and two midpoint checkers already down as targets in front of your checker: bar/18 is still an error there, about a tenth of a point. Only the weak board moves the number much; the targets hardly do. The race argument does not apply at all. Eighteen pips down, no play with a 6-1 makes you a racing favourite, and the checker you were hurrying is the one that has to stay back. When you are far behind in the race with one checker back, that checker's job is to be safe and patient, and the rest of your army's job is to keep a board for the day the shot comes. The bar point would be the place to be if X's board were two points and X had nowhere to land; then the exchange of hits favours you. It is not the place to be against four points and an 8-point.\n"
